\osc creates an OSC client.
Its syntax is the following:

\osc myosc

Once created, the client must be set up with the IP and
the port. This is done with the following:

\myosc.setup 127.0.0.1 8050

The line above sets the client to use the local IP of the
machine where LiveLily is running. In such a case, the IP
can be omitted and only the port can be set, or the
keyword localhost can be used.
The line below sets the client to send data to port 8000
at localhost (or 127.0.0.1)

\myosc.setup 8000

Once setup, the client can send messages with its send
command. It can take any number and type of arguments.
For example, the line below is correct and will send a
float, an integer and a string.
Take care to write the OSC address to send to as the
first argument, inside quotes, otherwise you'll get an
error.

\myosc.send "/addr" 3.14 57 "hello world"
